{"result": {"response": {"id": "chatcmpl-7d5356ac-280f-903d-a407-edbd60e1f64b", "created": 1773055117, "model": "qwen-flash", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "{\n    \"findings\": [\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"The International Headache Society (IHS) is the central governing body responsible for developing, maintaining, and disseminating the International Classification of Headache Disorders, 3rd edition (ICHD-3), which serves as the global standard for diagnosing and categorizing headache disorders. This organization has been instrumental in advancing headache medicine since its founding, with the first classification published in 1988 and subsequent updates, including the current ICHD-3, which is widely referenced in clinical and research settings. The IHS's authority is reinforced by its direct authorship and publication of the ICHD-3, as well as its foundational role in establishing the classification standard that underpins modern headache diagnosis. Its influence extends globally, with the ICHD-3 being adopted as the definitive diagnostic framework in both medical practice and scientific literature. The society's ongoing stewardship ensures consistency and accuracy in the classification of headache conditions, making it a critical entity in the community. [Data: Entities (29, 89); Relationships (103, 33, 487, +more)]\",\n            \"summary\": \"The International Headache Society as the authoritative body behind ICHD-3\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"The International Classification of Headache Disorders, 3rd edition (ICHD-3) is the definitive diagnostic manual and global standard for classifying all headache disorders, including migraine and its numerous subtypes. It provides detailed, evidence-based criteria for diagnosing conditions such as migraine without aura, migraine with aura, chronic migraine, menstrual migraine, vestibular migraine, and various complications like status migrainosus and migrainous infarction. The ICHD-3 is not only a reference for clinical diagnosis but also a foundational resource for research, guideline development, and treatment planning. Its comprehensive structure enables consistent classification across diverse medical settings, ensuring that healthcare providers worldwide use a common language to identify and manage headache disorders. The document is routinely cited in both academic and clinical contexts, underscoring its central role in the community. [Data: Entities (30, 89, 462, 492); Relationships (34, 40, 603, +more)]\",\n            \"summary\": \"ICHD-3 as the global diagnostic standard for headache disorders\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"Migraine with aura and migraine without aura are two core subtypes of migraine that are formally classified within the ICHD-3 framework. Migraine without aura is characterized by recurrent headaches lasting 4–72 hours, with features such as unilateral location, pulsating quality, moderate to severe intensity, and aggravation by physical activity, often accompanied by nausea, vomiting, photophobia, and phonophobia. Migraine with aura, in contrast, is defined by the presence of reversible neurological symptoms—such as visual disturbances, sensory changes, speech difficulties, or brainstem symptoms—occurring before or during the headache. These two subtypes can coexist in the same patient, reflecting the complexity of migraine as a condition. The ICHD-3 provides distinct diagnostic criteria for each, enabling accurate differentiation and appropriate treatment planning. [Data: Entities (79, 80); Relationships (34, 84, 88, +more)]\",\n            \"summary\": \"Core migraine subtypes defined by ICHD-3\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"The ICHD-3 includes four specific subtypes of migraine with aura: typical aura migraine, retinal migraine, brainstem aura migraine, and hemiplegic migraine. Each subtype is defined by unique neurological symptoms and diagnostic criteria. Typical aura migraine involves fully reversible visual, sensory, or speech/language symptoms without motor, brainstem, or retinal involvement. Retinal migraine is characterized by monocular visual disturbances such as flashes, scotomas, or temporary blindness, confirmed by clinical examination. Brainstem aura migraine, formerly known as basilar migraine, features symptoms originating from the brainstem, such as vertigo, dysarthria, tinnitus, and decreased consciousness, with a Glasgow Coma Scale score of 13 or less indicating altered mental status. Hemiplegic migraine is a rare, familial form associated with mutations in genes like CACNA1A, ATP1A2, and SCN1A, and is marked by temporary motor weakness. These subtypes are clinically significant due to their distinct presentations and management implications, including contraindications for certain medications like triptans. [Data: Entities (464, 465, 167, 171); Relationships (564, 565, 566, 567, 568, 569, 563, +more)]\",\n            \"summary\": \"Subtypes of migraine with aura and their clinical significance\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"Chronic migraine is a severe and disabling form of migraine defined by headache occurring on 15 or more days per month for more than three months, with at least eight of those days exhibiting migraine-specific features. It represents a progression from episodic migraine and is associated with significant functional impairment and reduced quality of life. The ICHD-3 provides clear diagnostic criteria, including the presence of moderate to severe pulsating pain, unilateral location, aggravation by activity, and associated symptoms like nausea and sensitivity to light and sound. Chronic migraine often coexists with other conditions such as medication overuse headache and new daily persistent headache, which can complicate diagnosis and treatment. The condition is a major focus in clinical practice due to its high burden and the need for comprehensive management strategies. [Data: Entities (33, 113, 111); Relationships (136, 137, 138, +more)]\",\n            \"summary\": \"Chronic migraine as a high-burden condition\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"Menstrual migraine is a proposed diagnostic category in the ICHD-3, categorized into three subtypes: pure menstrual migraine, menstrually-related migraine, and non-menstrual migraine. Pure menstrual migraine occurs exclusively during the perimenstrual window (days -2 to +3 of menstruation), while menstrually-related migraine occurs during this period but also at other times. Non-menstrual migraine refers to attacks unrelated to the menstrual cycle. This classification helps identify hormonal triggers and tailor treatment strategies, such as prophylactic therapy during specific phases of the cycle. Although currently listed in the appendix and requiring further validation, menstrual migraine is clinically recognized and supported by evidence linking hormonal fluctuations to migraine onset. Its inclusion in the ICHD-3 framework highlights the importance of considering reproductive health in headache management. [Data: Entities (34, 488, 489, 490); Relationships (605, 606, 607, 608, +more)]\",\n            \"summary\": \"Menstrual migraine and its subtypes in clinical context\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"Vestibular migraine is a recognized but proposed diagnostic entity in the ICHD-3, characterized by recurrent episodes of vertigo, dizziness, and imbalance that are temporally related to migraine attacks. These episodes are often accompanied by typical migraine features such as headache, photophobia, phonophobia, or visual aura. The condition is distinct from other vestibular disorders and is diagnosed based on specific clinical criteria. Despite its clinical relevance, vestibular migraine remains in the appendix of the ICHD-3, indicating that further research is needed to solidify its diagnostic boundaries. However, it is widely acknowledged in clinical practice and is a focus of ongoing scientific investigation. The presence of vestibular symptoms is a key diagnostic feature, and their identification is crucial for accurate diagnosis and treatment. [Data: Entities (35, 491); Relationships (604, 608, +more)]\",\n            \"summary\": \"Vestibular migraine as a proposed but clinically relevant condition\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"Migraine complications are severe or prolonged conditions that arise as a result of migraine attacks and are formally classified in the ICHD-3. These include status migrainosus (a migraine lasting more than 72 hours), persistent aura without infarction (aura lasting one week or more without stroke), migrainous infarction (stroke occurring during a migraine aura), and migraine aura-triggered seizure. These complications represent a significant clinical burden and require urgent medical attention. The ICHD-3 provides specific diagnostic criteria for each, enabling early recognition and intervention. The existence of these complications underscores the potential severity of migraine beyond episodic pain, highlighting the need for comprehensive management and monitoring. [Data: Entities (42, 43, 44, 45, 46); Relationships (49, 50, 51, 52, +more)]\",\n            \"summary\": \"Migraine complications and their clinical implications\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"Episodic syndromes that may be associated with migraine are a category in the ICHD-3 that includes recurrent disorders often seen in children and potentially linked to migraine pathophysiology. These include recurrent gastrointestinal disturbance, cyclical vomiting syndrome, abdominal migraine, benign paroxysmal vertigo, and benign paroxysmal torticollis. These conditions are characterized by stereotypical, repetitive episodes and are considered potential precursors or manifestations of migraine. Their inclusion in the ICHD-3 framework supports the idea that migraine is a broader neurological spectrum disorder that can present in diverse ways, especially in pediatric populations. Recognizing these syndromes can aid in early diagnosis and intervention, potentially preventing progression to more chronic forms of migraine. [Data: Entities (48, 49, 50, 51, 52, 53); Relationships (53, 54, 55, 56, 57, +more)]\",\n            \"summary\": \"Episodic syndromes associated with migraine in pediatric populations\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"Specialized headache centers and headache专病门诊 (specialized outpatient clinics) are key clinical settings for the advanced diagnosis and management of complex headache disorders. These facilities are equipped to handle detailed diagnostic evaluations, including fourth- and fifth-layer assessments, and are particularly important for conditions like migraine with aura, chronic migraine, and vestibular migraine. They provide multidisciplinary care, integrating neurology, pain management, and psychological support to address the multifaceted nature of headache disorders. The close relationship between these centers and the ICHD-3 framework ensures that diagnoses are consistent with international standards. Their existence reflects the growing recognition of headache disorders as serious medical conditions requiring specialized care. [Data: Entities (82, 81); Relationships (88, 91, +more)]\",\n            \"summary\": \"Specialized headache centers as advanced diagnostic and treatment hubs\"\n        }\n    ],\n    \"rating\": 9.5,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity rating is high due to the global clinical and research significance of the ICHD-3 and its role in standardizing the diagnosis and treatment of migraine and other headache disorders, which affect millions worldwide.\",\n    \"summary\": \"The community centers around the International H